<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#millet">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Millet</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Millet is a group of hardy, drought-tolerant cereal grains grown widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. It was a staple crop of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the Han and Ming dynasties and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> states of Africa, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> states. Its resilience made it essential to agricultural economies in dry regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millet.jpg" alt="Millet" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kurt Stüber [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panicum_miliaceum0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
